{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>When a man dies in a tent.</b> The same law applies to a house, but Scripture mentions 'tent' because Israel in the wilderness were dwellers in tents, and the verse is an admonishment for that time and for all generations. From here it follows that they do not impart tum'ah in a tent except Israel, who are called 'adam (man),' as it is said (Ezekiel 34:31): ״אדם אתםם — 'You are adam.' And even though we find concerning a non-Jew (Numbers 31:40): ״ונפש אדם ששה עשר אלף״ — 'and human souls, sixteen thousand' — they already explained there in Yevamot, chapter HaBa Al Yevemto, that there it is on account of the animal. And so too what is written (Jonah 4:11): ״רבו אדם אשר לא ידע״ — 'more than one hundred and twenty thousand adam who do not know,' etc. — there too they explained it on account of the animal, meaning that the nations are nowhere called 'adam' alone unless Scripture mentions 'behemah' (animal). And what is written (Psalms 124:2): ״בקום עלינו אדם״ — 'when adam rose against us' — they already interpreted it in the first chapter of Megillah as referring to a non-king. And what is written (Isaiah 43:4): ״ואתן אדם תחתיך״ — 'and I give adam in your stead' — they interpreted in Berakhot: do not read 'adam' but 'Edom.' And what is written (Leviticus 18:5): ״אשר יעשה אותם האדם״ — 'which the adam shall do' — and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted: it does not say 'kohen, levi, and Yisrael' but 'ha-adam,' teaching that even a gentile who engages in Torah is like the High Priest — so they interpreted in Sanhedrin. But they explained this matter as deriving from the superfluous letter heh, which comes to include even a gentile, for it did not say ״אשר יעשה אותם אדם״ but ״האדם״. And what Jeremiah said (Jeremiah 32:20): ״אשר שמת אותות ומופתים בארץ מצרים ובישראל ובאדם״ — 'who set signs and wonders in the land of Egypt and in Israel and in adam' — this refers to the Egyptian 'adam,' because those plagues were punishments." "<b>All who come into the tent.</b> Is tamei, and all the more so one who carries the dead person, since concerning a carcass it is written (Leviticus 11:40): ״והנושא את נבלתה״ — 'and one who carries its carcass.' Now if a carcass, which does not impart seven-day tum'ah, yet imparts tum'ah by carrying — how much more so a dead person, who imparts seven-day tum'ah, should impart tum'ah by carrying. But Scripture remained silent about tum'ah by carrying in the case of a dead person because it is a kal va-homer (a fortiori) from 'tent': if all who come into the tent are tamei, then certainly the one who carries. And Scripture remained silent just as it remained silent regarding the prohibition of a daughter, which is a kal va-homer from a daughter's daughter; or the prohibition of eating meat with milk, which is a kal va-homer from cooking."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Ezekiel 34:31 verse text reads ואתם צאן מרעיתי אדם אתם; I rendered the two words Rabbeinu Bahya quotes as they appear in his citation. The prooftext chain is preserved in the order given; b. Yevamot 60b–61a is the referenced discussion."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:34:07.877333582Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}